# 6. AI SKIN SCAN AND COMPUTER VISION

The existing AI Skin Scan must become a meaningful, reliable feature rather than a visual demonstration.

## Existing model

The project is expected to use a YOLOv8n-based skin analysis pipeline.

First inspect the actual model, weights, dataset, preprocessing, inference pipeline, backend, and existing prediction logic.

Never assume that a model is accurate simply because it returns a prediction.

## Required improvements

Evaluate:

- Dataset quality.
- Label consistency.
- Class imbalance.
- Training and validation splits.
- Data leakage.
- Image quality.
- Lighting variation.
- Skin tone diversity.
- Camera differences.
- Model confidence calibration.
- Precision.
- Recall.
- F1-score.
- Confusion matrix.
- Per-class performance.
- Inference latency.
- Generalization to unseen images.

Improve the model only when sufficient data and technical resources are available.

Do not fabricate evaluation metrics.

Do not claim that model accuracy has improved without a reproducible evaluation.

Do not generate fake skin-analysis results.

## User experience

Implement:

1. Camera capture.
2. Image upload.
3. Image quality assessment.
4. Face and skin region detection where supported.
5. Image preprocessing.
6. AI inference.
7. Prediction confidence.
8. Visual result presentation.
9. Explainable prediction summaries.
10. Recommended skincare actions.
11. Relevant Thorakao product recommendations.
12. Analysis history where users explicitly consent.

Provide clear explanations of the model's limitations.

Do not diagnose dermatological diseases.

Do not claim medical-grade accuracy.

Do not use image-based predictions to make unsupported medical or health claims.

Protect facial images and other personal data. Obtain explicit consent before storing images.

